Subject: [PATCH] usb: hub: skip the connect debounce on the OTG root port
Upstream-Status: Pending

hub_port_connect() waits for 100ms of stable connection in 25ms steps
(hub_port_debounce_be_stable()) before it lets enumeration begin. That
exists for mechanical contacts. On the OTG root port of a role-switch
platform the connection is protocol-controlled: the peer switched roles
at our request and pulled up D+ on a cable that carried traffic a
moment earlier. Meanwhile the peer is a device waiting for a bus reset
with a short fuse (~200ms on a Continental NAC Wave 2), and the
debounce alone eats half of it.

0032 already skips the hub_activate() debounce on this port for the
same reason; skip the per-connect one too, using the same
hub_is_b_host_otg_port() test. The port status is re-read once so a
device that vanished in the meantime is still seen, and
hub_port_init() handles a disconnect during reset as before.

Car (NAC Wave 2), together with the previous patch: the first role
switch after boot succeeds, which it never did before.

---
 drivers/usb/core/hub.c | 33 ++++++++++++++++++++++++---------
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/usb/core/hub.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/core/hub.c
@@ -5427,16 +5427,36 @@
 
 	if (portchange & (USB_PORT_STAT_C_CONNECTION |
 				USB_PORT_STAT_C_ENABLE)) {
-		status = hub_port_debounce_be_stable(hub, port1);
-		if (status < 0) {
-			if (status != -ENODEV &&
-				port1 != unreliable_port &&
-				printk_ratelimit())
-				dev_err(&port_dev->dev, "connect-debounce failed\n");
-			portstatus &= ~USB_PORT_STAT_CONNECTION;
-			unreliable_port = port1;
+		if (hub_is_b_host_otg_port(hdev, port1)) {
+			/*
+			 * Protocol-controlled connection on the OTG root port:
+			 * the peer switched roles on our request and pulled up
+			 * D+ on the same cable that was stable a moment ago.
+			 * There is no contact bounce to wait out, and the peer
+			 * is now a device waiting for a bus reset with a short
+			 * fuse. Take the current status and move on; a real
+			 * disconnect is caught by hub_port_init() anyway.
+			 */
+			u16 pc;
+
+			usb_clear_port_feature(hub->hdev, port1,
+					       USB_PORT_FEAT_C_CONNECTION);
+			status = usb_hub_port_status(hub, port1, &portstatus,
+						     &pc);
+			if (status < 0)
+				portstatus &= ~USB_PORT_STAT_CONNECTION;
 		} else {
-			portstatus = status;
+			status = hub_port_debounce_be_stable(hub, port1);
+			if (status < 0) {
+				if (status != -ENODEV &&
+					port1 != unreliable_port &&
+					printk_ratelimit())
+					dev_err(&port_dev->dev, "connect-debounce failed\n");
+				portstatus &= ~USB_PORT_STAT_CONNECTION;
+				unreliable_port = port1;
+			} else {
+				portstatus = status;
+			}
 		}
 	}
 
